        <body>
            <div xml:id="d37">

                <pb facs="0055" n="49" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg49"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Sill to Mr.
                            Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 38.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Seoul, Korea</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-08-07"><hi rendition="#italic">August 7, 1894</hi></date>.
                        (Received September 12.)</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that I, last
                    evening, received a notice from the Japanese minister here, as per copy
                    inclosed, of the formal declaration of war by Japan against China on August 1.
                    This notice was accompanied by a private note (see copy) informing me that <pb
                        facs="0056" n="50" xml:id="pg50"/> Japanese consulates, their legation and
                    subjects in China, would be under the protection of the United States in
                    consequence of the cessasation of peaceful conditions.</p>
                <p>I at once communicated this intelligence to Captain Day, of the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Baltimore</hi>, at Chemulpo, the senior naval officer
                    present.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John M. B. Sill.</hi></signed></closer>



